TEARING DOWN DISSENT
 Epidemic of Poster-Ripping at UCSD

 taken from Campus Report, Accuracy In Academia, July-August 1991

  Calling quotas _institutionalized racism,_ the Young Americans
 for Freedom (YAF) chapter at the University of California at San
 Diego began  postering the campus to point out problems that
 race-based admission standards have on the University of
 California system.  In response, YAF members were accused of
 alleged racism as their posters were systematically ripped down,
 and the university did nothing to condemn these actions.
  UCSD-YAF created a flyer, titled _Quotas Over Quality,_ that
 compared the academic and extracurricular credentials of Jennifer
 Reil, a real student, and a hypothetical student from an
 _underrepresented population_ named John Doe.  Doe, with a 2.96
 GPA and 539 SAT score, was accepted over Reil_s actual 4.5 GPA and
 1110 SAT.
  The dispute between YAF and other student organizations,
 including the Student Affirmative Action Committee, the Women_s
 Resource Center, and the African-American Student Organization,
 began on May 4th when YAF posted 300 flyers on the campus
 questioning UCSD_s "affirmative action" and admissions policies.
 The posters were quickly torn down by student activists opposing
 YAF's position who reportedly claimed they had a right to tear
 down the posters.  They claimed this right because YAF did not
 have a student government stamp required of groups in order to
 have their posters and flyers distributed on the campus.  YAF
 leaders say that this later found out that no such stamp existed.
  Eleven days later, 300 more flyers were posted.  This new set of
 posters was also torn down.  On this occasion, students destroying
 the posters said that they were doing so because YAF was not a
 recognized student organization.  The YAF chapter, however, had
 been commissioned as an official organization at noon on the day
 the posters were posted.  When a complaint was filed with the UCSD
 administration, organizations advisor Marcia Strong reportedly
 told YAF: _student will be students._  In hopes of putting an end
 to the misconception about YAF_s status with the university, YAF
 informed other organizations that they had the right to post
 flyers.
  The third wave of the postering campaign took place on May 20th,
 with 300 more flyers posted.  Students removing them now claimed
 they did so because the posters _weren_t factual._
  Jennifer Reil, the actual student in the the YAF poster, was the
 subject of an article in the San Diego Union and in Filipino
 newspapers in the area.  She is a high school student of
 second-generation Filipino descent who was rejected by University
 of California at Berkeley despite the fact that she was ranked at
 the top of her class with a better-than-perfect GPA and strong
 extracurriculars like cheerleading and the schools academic
 decathlon team.  Students with lower test scores and GPAs in her
 high school class had been admitted.  Berkeley claims that Reil
 was rejected on the grounds that she was applying to the very
 competitive engineering program where few positions are available,
 and said that Reil_s SAT scores did not coincide with those of
 students qualified for the program.
  In an attempt to stop the destruction of its posters, YAF met
 with the Student Affirmative Action Director Anthony Jemison to
 discuss the issues of censorship on the part of other students.
 Jemison reportedly made his opposition to the chapter_s position
 on affirmative action clear, and became _irate_ when YAF asked him
 to talk to the organizations suspected of ripping down posters
 about the problem.  Jemison allegedly said students tearing down
 posters were not representing their organizations by their
 actions.  Jemison argued that YAF had the _responsibility to make
 the information more accurate,_ and provided them with definitions
 of _quota_ and _goal._  It was also claimed that Jemison attempted
 to _belittle their opinions_ by covering up issues with
 politically correct jargon.
  In a letter to UCSD Vice Chancellor Joseph Watson, YAF Vice
 Chairman Peter Kanelos wrote: _YAF believes Mr. Jemison did
 discuss the issue of our flyers in his meeting with SAAC and his
 manner of doing so gave the student organizations the University_s
 tacit approval to censor YAF_s position.  Mr. Jemisons_
 interpretation of the accuracy of YAF_s flyers was his method of
 giving the SAAC his approval of censoring ideas which were not
 conducive to their organization_s cause.  It is our understanding
 that the areas reserved for posting flyers is property of the
 University and therefore no one but the University has the right
 to remove flyers._  Kanelos also rhetorically asked: _Who
 determines the accuracy of political statements on campus and for
 what purpose?  Big Brother?_
  Kanelos, explaining the postering campaign, wrote: _These flyers
 were created simply to make students ponder the possibilities of
 academic quotas.  YAF knows the University claims to have "goals"
 and not quotas, but it is YAF_s belief that these goals have
 become de facto quotas.  This disagreement certainly does not give
 the University the right to censor our speech._
  YAF held a press conference on May 20th to address the issues of
 _politically correct thought_ and racially discriminatory
 admissions standards.  YAF, in addition, promised to post 600 of
 the flyers before the press conference began.  YAF asked the
 press: _We are not here today to see who has the best statistics,
 or to get in a battle over semantics. We are here to pose a
 question: Are quotas right or wrong?_
  A San Diego Union  editorial argued for YAF_s rights.  Noting
 that Jemison and Watson both showed _lukewarm_ attitudes toward
 the posters, the editorial read: _Whether one agrees or disagrees
 with the YAF position is irrelevant.  The student group must be
 accorded the same freedom to express itself as any other campus
 organization, whether liberal or conservative....Competing points
 of view, no matter how controversial, are the lifeblood of
 democracy.  Institutions of higher education, in particular,
 should be citadels of free speech.  College administrators should
 be mindful that freedom of expression on campus cannot exist when
 officials misuse their authority to silence a controversial
 speaker._
----------------------------------------------------------------
                    WHAT IS AIA?
     Accuracy in Academia, a nonprofit public service organization based in 
Washington, D.C., seeks the reassertion of traditional academic ethics in our 
universities. We contend that academic freedom is threatened by a progressive 
ideological orthodoxy, pervasive in the intellectual community, which degrades 
professional standards in teaching and scholarship, and inhibits speech and 
research which contradict orthodox views.
     AIA wishes to see the academy return to its proper mission: the quest for 
truth. To promote our goal, we document and publicize political bias within the
academy in our monthly newspaper, "Campus Report". Our articles focus on three 
issues: the exploitation of the classroom or university resources to 
indoctrinate students; discrimination against students, faculty or 
administrators based on political or academic beliefs; and campus violations of
free speech.
