NEWS RELEASE

Santa Rosa Junior College Oak Leaf
1501 Mendocino Ave
Santa Rosa, CA 95401 

CONTACT: The Oak Leaf Crisis Committee
Troy Petersen, Editor-in-Chief
Robin Flinchum, Student Advisor
Tammy Wittler, Entertainment Editor
Linda Morgan, Assistant News Editor
Lori Bryan, Opinion Editor

707-527-4401



May 4, 1993

On April 30, Roger Karraker, faculty advisor for the Santa Rosa
Junior College newspaper, The Oak Leaf, was placed on partial
administrative leave with full pay, pending completion of an investigation
involving a computer conferencing system.  According to a letter from Dr.
Robert Agrella, president of SRJC, this action was taken as a result of "a
claim of sexual harassment in the journalism program." 
Agrella's decision results from a complicated chain of events
involving alleged remarks posted on the system by what seems to be less
than a half-dozen individuals, some who are members of the Oak Leaf staff. 
Super Oak Leaf Online, or SOLO as the conferencing system is
known, plays a vital role in the production of The Oak Leaf as a
communication and organization tool. However, we feel it should not be
considered as one and the same with The Oak Leaf. Many members of the SRJC
community who are not connected with The Oak Leaf use this system.
According to the SRJC administration, the investigation focuses
strictly on SOLO. Karraker has been temporarily relieved of his
responsibilities as faculty advisor to The Oak Leaf and instructor of a
beginning journalism class, but curiously was not removed as instructor of
Journalism 399.2, a class which deals exclusively with SOLO and
telecommunications.
In Karraker's absence, The Oak Leaf staff has been assigned a
faculty advisor who is not familiar with the electronic communication
issues that are at the core of this controversy. In addition, the
instructor substituting for Karraker in the journalism class is not a
journalism instructor, but specializes in English and technical writing.
While we do not condone any form of denigration or personal
attacks on SOLO, we feel the need as students of journalism to defend the
fundamental right to freedom of expression. We acknowledge that harmful
attitudes towards women exist in society, but we feel that these issues
cannot be confronted and resolved if only some of us are free to speak.
Furthermore, we do not believe that Karraker should be held
responsible for any remarks made by a few individuals who do not
necessarily represent his attitudes towards women. Karraker has been a
champion of free speech throughout his career. He has instilled in us a
profound respect for this fundamental liberty and the need for us to
exercise that right with dignity and responsibility.
We demand that our advisor be reinstated in full and that the
administration of SRJC cease its denial of our basic right to an
education. We will not stand by quietly and watch a man who we trust and
respect become a sacrificial lamb in the name of any organization, no
matter what its principles or agenda. 
-- 
