Carl's reply is, as usual, valuable.  Many college catalogs include
reasonable summaries of FERPA, as well.  Have a look at yours.

Was there anything concrete that concerned you?  As I understand it,
if, for instance, your school configured finger so that it disclosed
your "education records" (in the vernacular) without your
authorization, then the school would be in violation of FERPA. Surely,
though, they have done nothing so silly, have they?

Recently, two sorts of cases involving FERPA have made news.  One class
involves suits by campus papers to receive access to campus police
records.  Holdings have been that FERPA does not prohibit such access.
(That's what many administrations have held.)  The other is a suit vs.
Rutgers University over use of SSN as id # and publication of it on
registration lists and posted grades.  In short, as I recall it, the
holding was that posting grades by SSN is a violation and must not be
permitted; rearranging the bureaucracy was held to be an undue
administrative burden and so not required.  As I recall.  And I'm not
an attorney, thank you very much.  :)

