Subject: Are calls for rights protection a slam against sys admins?
Message-ID: <1993May6.183757.3943@eff.org>
Date: Thu, 6 May 1993 18:37:57 GMT

I recently received email that said "I dislike the constant
implication that system administrators can only be restrained by fear
of punishment, and will invariably act with malice whenever possible)".

Here is my reply:

I do not intent to imply this.

I don't believe that sys admins can only be restrained by fear of
punishment or that they will invariably (or even usually) act with
malice. You should not take my advocacy for clear policies with
explict protections as slam against sys admins any more than you
should take the First Amendment as a slam against the President Geroge
Washington or your univerity's free expression and due process policy
as a slam against the campus administration.

Put another way, I believe in the rule of law over the rule of man.
But I also believe that good rules/laws need seldom be enforced.

Moreover, I'm confident that sys admins will more and more become the
chief advocates, defenders, and propagaters of intellectual freedom
and IF policy. (Just as librarians are for libraries and professors
are for universities-at-large).

- Carl
