                       RAs: Minions of Multiculturalism

                              by Danielle S. Allen
                                     Senior
                              Princeton University

     Princeton has residential advisors, the University of Alabama at
Birmingham resident assistants, the University of California at San Diego
house advisors, and Middlebury College Junior counselors, but whatever their 
names, these upperclass students who live among their peers serve as the 
extension of the administration into residential life. As such, residential 
assistants (or RAs as they are referred to throughout this article) pass 
through training programs and undertake duties that mirror the variety and 
trends present in the goals of the administrations of America's campuses.

     At Cornell, RAs leam to counsel and live with freshmen by attending a
week long "training session" before school starts. According to Timothy
Gregory, an RA at Cornell, very little of that session focuses on any
practical matter associated with being a residential assistant. In 1991, for 
instance, the new RAs began their training by discussing minorities in higher 
education, multiculturalism, and oppression. "We played Simon Says," Gregory 
said recalling one training session activity. "We were told, for example, that 
you were born a white female or something. All the 'white males' stepped 
forward. Everyone else winds up in the back. Then you had to define racism."

     On the third day of the training session students learned about AIDS,
HIV, and about homophobia. "They showed us two movies,...the second one was a 
gay safe sex video that was basically triple X. You see them engaged in all 
kinds of sex acts. They used a condom doused with honey," says Gregory. Similar
issues relating to sex and self-defense followed. Instruction on matters like 
fire safety and dorm rules, as well as a mere three-hour role playing activity,
did not surface until the final days of RA "training" school.

     In their quest to make the dorms hospitable to "diverse" student
populations, campus housing officials often alienate the average student. For 
example, at Vanderbilt, Cornell, and dozens of other schools, RAs were given a 
list of questions to ask their residents which included, "If you are a 
heterosexual, is it possible that all you need is a good gay lover?" The 
University of Maryland is perhaps guilty of the most egregious case of 
hyper-sensitivity -- during the Gulf War, in order not to offend Arab students,
the administration required students to remove American flags that had been 
hung from dorm windows.

     RAs at many schools are not allowed to place Christmas, Hanukkah, or
other religious holiday greetings on their doors or bulletin boards. Requests 
by RAs to attend Sunday religious services during RA training week at Cornell 
were denied. While sensitive toward some groups, housing officials at many 
schools are thoughtless toward others -- one speaker said during Cornell's RA 
training week, "Roman Catholics have really done a number on this planet."

     Says Gregory of the Cornell training experience, "What they did the
first three-days had nothing to do with what I actually encountered when
students arrived. I had to counsel a lot of roommate disputes the first week, 
but it wasn't until later in the year that we had in-services on conflict 
mediation. I needed it before students arrived."

     At the University of Alabama at Birmingham (UAB) another week long 
pre-semester training program prepares RAs much differently -- directing nearly
all its time toward instruction on practical problems. According to Pam 
Pilcher, a former resident assistant and present residence hall director, the 
first day of training begins with "Hard Basic Facts" (for example, RAs are 
required to hold office hours). "The second and third day we'll do team 
building," remarked Pilcher. "You have all the RAs trying to solve a problem 
together....On the 4th day we're having.. .professional[s] come and ta]k about 
counseling, about alcohol and drug abuse, AIDS awareness, sexually transmitted 
disease (STD) information, and how to recognize problems. On the fifth day, 
we'll do role playing. You learn procedures to handle situations you've never 
dealt with before,land how to assess them."

     The differences in RA training programs stem from the differing roles
that university administrations believe RAs should play within the
residential community. Some administrations, like the one at Cornell, are
most concerned with training RAs for the duty of promoting social education.

     Stanford resident fellow Dennis Matthies recently remarked that, "We are 
an educational institution, and as such we promote certain basic values. Some 
of these values are at odds with prevailing conditions in our society. That is 
our current situation as we seek to reduce on this campus...continued 
discrimination and verbal abuse targeted at gays, women, minorities, 'wimps,' 
and 'nerds."' Another RA at Stanford, Cecilia Burciaga, remarks, "I think we at
Stanford have not realized how unique our program is. Other universities have a
housing office or a dorm office. We have Residential Education." The goal of 
the housing program at schools like Stanford and Cornell is to extend 
education, particularly "social" education, into the residence halls.

     According to the job description at Princeton, RAs are expected "to
enforce the rules and regulations of the University; ... to be sensitive to and
respectful of the rights and individuality of each student, and to
encourage this outlook in others; to work cooperatively with the Minority
Affairs Advisers to enhance the environment at Princeton for African-
American, Latino, Asian-American and Native American students; to help create a
safe and hospitable climate for members of the gay, lesbian, and bisexual 
communities; [and] to attend and support college sponsored events and 
activities." What specific rules RAs must enforce are few. For example, there 
are no set quiet hours or rules about overnight guests. As with Cornell and 
Stanford, the emphasis is on "constructing" community.

     Specific regulations are much more important at schools like UAB, where 
the program is only a few years old, that remain closer to the original 
guidelines of in loco parentis (in the place of a parent). According to 
Pilcher, "We deal with maintenance, roommate problems, roommate conflicts.

     You're on on-call duty. You have office hours. RAs are expected to report 
violations of policy, i.e., Fire Alarms, Fireworks, Guns, Quiet Hours. Also, 
overnight guests must be of the same sex and may stay only if approved by an RA
and your roommates and if there's a bed."

     Daniel Buska, a former RA at Saint Leo College in Florida, describes his 
duties similarly: "We had to make sure everyone was behaving. We had to walk 
through the halls once an hour. We were responsible for locked doors, that 
music wasn't being played too loudly, and that students weren't being rowdy." 
Buska also remarked, "RAs were available for counseling, but most of the time 
we were supposed to send students to professionals."

     Despite the range of appoaches and goals, residential advisor programs do 
share one thing in common -- they all emphasize "programming," that is, socal 
events or educational programs sponsored by RAs. As with the training programs,
the content of these social events and educational programs differs according 
to the goals of each university administration.

     Tim Gregory at Cornell was required to put on one social and two
educational programs every month. "In the beginning of the year, during
training we set an agenda for the entire year. They [the administration]
wanted to balance the programs. In February, you stressed black issues. In 
April, gay issues, etc."

     Today's RAs act much like house mothers did in the early '60s. These
women -- older widows or unmarried women -- lived in dormitories and earned a 
full salary in addition to their housingaccommodations.Theageand salaries of 
house mothers marked them clearly as members of the administration who lived in
the dormitories.

     Intended to regulate the community life of their schools, house mothers 
operated in accordance with the governing principle of American universities: 
in locoparentis.Theymade sure doors were locked, students were in by curfew, 
guests were checked in and out (and if necessary, kicked out), and quiet hours 
were observed.

     As in loco parentis went out of fashion, schools stopped replacing their 
retiring house mothers. At the University of Wisconsin-Madison, they were gone 
by the mid-'60s. At University of Alabama-Birmingham, most of the house mothers
were gone by the late '80s, but today, the oldest dorm still has two full-time 
employees in charge of the building.

     Administrations did not, however, relinquish their own role as
regulators of dormitory life. Administrative representatives of a parental 
nature were simply supplanted by upperclassmen and graduate students.

     Most RAs are in fact, as were the house mothers, employees of their
schools. Most commonly, as at Scripps College, a woman's school in Claremont, 
California, administrations provide their RAs with room and board, or a portion
of the board given in the form of a monthly stipend. At Scripps, the RAs may 
not hold another part-time working position. Some schools, like UAB, also offer
perks such as free use of a campus phone.

     It is in exchange for room and board that RAs uphold the rules and
regulations of the university in the residence halls. Just as the house
mothers enforced in loco parentis, RAs enforce the intent of their
administrations, whether that be an updated version of in loco parentis, a plan
that incorporates older or alternative students, or a vision of how community 
members should interact with one another. As Frank Kelley, director of 
residential life at Middlebury College, said, "Basically, the junior counselors
are the eyes and ears of the deans in the residence hall."

     Whether they are organizing a sensitivity seminar, a sexually
transmitted disease workshop, a multicultural awareness program, a condom
distribution campaign, or checking in overnight guests, RAs are the hired
guns of the administration. While some college houses are certainly more
agreeable to live in than others, students will not escape the long arm of the 
administration until alternative forms of colle~e livin~ can be found.

