Overall use in the United States is governed by the acceptable use policy
published by NSFNet.  You can get this via anonymous ftp from:

    <NIC.MERIT.EDU> /nsfnet/acceptable.use.policies/nsfnet.txt

The short "official" answer to your question is at the end of this text:

   UNACCEPTABLE USES:
 
   (10) Use for for-profit activities, unless covered by the General 
        Principle or as a specifically acceptable use.
 
   (11) Extensive use for private or personal business.

This is taken out of context, so get the whole thing.

Also, be aware that you are probably governed by regional and/or local
network or community acceptable use policies.  Here in the mid-west, for
example, we are a member of MIDNET, which has an acceptable use policy even
more stringent than NSFNET's. The local campus has an acceptable use policy
for computers and networks.  In addition, the campus as a whole has a
"student handbook" with policies governing solicitation using campus
facilities.

So, there is no "one answer" for everyone, but at least now you know where
to start looking.

