This is completely irrelavent; there is no "governing body" for Usenet.

The Internet, and the NSFNet, is a TRANSPORT.  Usenet is a MEDIUM.  If you
receive a newsfeed from somewhere, you are responsible for the content that
you take or send via that path.

If you have connections via "commercial" Internet channels (ie: Alternet),
and you send commercial messages through Usenet, you have violated no
policy.  You cannot control <where> or <how> your message is transported
after it leaves your site (ie: your responsibility to abide by the AUPs for
Usenet is to the site you feed or send to).

Thus, for companies on networks such as Alternet or PSI, if their end-nodes
are non-AUP restricted, then the traffic is acceptable as far as they are
able to control and determine.  As an example, I am connected via Alternet,
and feed news to and from UUNET and EFF.ORG.  Both are on a non-AUP network.
I also feed via UUCP to and from a number of sites.  I can pass anything
over those links that we both agree to, <including> commercial postings, as
long as it is legal (passing stolen credit-card numbers would be a bad idea,
for obvious reasons).

It is also completely possible for people to run news on UUCP connected
sites (and many, many of them do).  None of these sites have to abide by 
any AUP.

Further, many AUP-network connected sites get "biz", which is EXPLICITLY
commercial.  

There are many networks which have no AUP restrictions, and many others
which do of varying forms.  None of that means a hill of beans in the
context of Usenet unless the site you're posting from is on an
AUP-restricted network.  If it is, you are likely in violation of that 
AUP in a technical sense already.  Its hard to argue that "alt.eat.me.now"
serves any research and education purpose.

All the world ain't funded out of taxpayer largesse.  

None of this will keep you from getting flamed by Usenet purists, however.

Technically, individuals posting for sale items in misc.forsale probably
violate the AUP of NSFNet -- there's nothing in that posting which has
anything to do with research and education.  

<Convention> says that businesses should post their items within the "biz"
heirarchy, except for short news announcements and items of general interest
which are not repeated.  Normal "advertisements" definitely belong in Biz --
but this is by convention, not policy or law.

