In article <skunz.736789469@corvette.cc.iastate.edu> skunz@iastate.edu
(Steven L Kunz) writes:
>Overall use in the United States is governed by the acceptable use policy
>published by NSFNet.

This is false, Steven.  99.9% of Usenet connections in the US do not
travel over NSFNet; most of the Internet is not NSFNet and has much looser
rules.  Most companies on the Internet are connected to a network that
either belongs to CIX (commercial Internet eXchange) or has similar
relaxed rules: yes, you CAN conduct business over the Internet provided
that you aren't doing it over NSFnet links, and it's no problem these days
to go around the world without ever touching NSFnet (UC Berkeley and
Stanford are not on NSFnet, BTW -- I have no idea whether Iowa State is so
your rules may be different).

In any case, NSF is in the process of relaxing the rules considerably (if
the rules are read literally, many folks have been in violation for
years).

There is no central control of Usenet.  Policy in the forsale and
marketplace groups is set by peer pressure and peer pressure alone.  The
tradition has been that these groups are for individuals selling things
and for one-time getting rid of stuff; regular businesses are expected to
pay for their advertising.  The reason is that many people and companies
pay for their newsfeed and get very pissed off about being forced to
subsidize someone's business.

Certain specialized groups have been set up explicitly for businesses to

	* announce new products: comp.newprod
	* conduct business: the "biz" hierarchy

People whose net access rules say they can't do this kind of thing can
simply not carry these groups.  The rest of us make it easier for these
folks by using the right groups to conduct business.

But there is no central authority to decide what is legit and what isn't.


