THIS ARTICLE APPEARED IN THE FALL 1992 ISSUE OF
THE INTERCOLLEGIATE REVIEW, VOLUME 28, NUMBER 1
COPYRIGHT INTERCOLLEGIATE STUDIES INSTITUTE, INC. 1992


                   INSIDE THE SENSITIVITY SESSION
                         ABRAHAM H. MILLER

     Toward the end of the 1991 Spring Quarter, Matt Ball, a staff
reporter for the University of Cincinnati's student newspaper, wrote a
series exposing "political correctness" on his campus, a campus situated
in one of the nation's most conservative cities. His stories were
greeted by some faculty and administrators with the usual doses of
professed disbelief and official denial. One professor in the English
department pointedly challenged the series' assertion that campus-based
"racial sensitivity training" was little more than harassment sessions
directed against white males.1 Such challenges usually go unanswered,
for few faculty members anywhere are willing to speak out against their
colleagues or their administration.

     But this particular challenge produced a response, and revealed
harsh truths about what happens inside sensitivity sessions. William
Daniels, a library employee, refused to let official denial be the last
word. In a guest column to the student newspaper, Daniels detailed his
own experience as part of a captive audience immersed in the ideological
coercion that, ironically, is called "sensitivity training." 2

     Daniels' exposure to multicultural awareness began with the
distribution of Barbara Ehrenreich's polemical essay on "cultural
diversity." Ehrenreich's essay set the tone for the session and
characterized the only point of view presented. Even before the session
had started, the library administration attempted to remove a set of
"politically incorrect" New Republic articles from the Reserve Room's
collection of readings for the training.

     After several women and minority group members shared their views
about the session, the group leader, Vice Provost Mary Ellen Ashley,
called everyone's attention to the behavior of the white males, who had
remained silent. She pointed out that their silence would be tolerated
for the present, but shortly the rules would change, and they would have
to participate.

     Participation was just what the next exercise demanded. Ashley
passed out paper and pencil and told the participants they would write
an essay, "What I can do to help our department demonstrate our
appreciation for diversity." She made it explicit that they were not to
write about what they were going to think or talk about but what they
were going to "do!" Needless to say, the possibility that someone was
not going to help his department appreciate diversity was beyond the
realm of options. The Vice Provost made these limitations indelibly
clear by directly stating that anyone who did not agree with the
university's diversity policy should consider getting a job elsewhere.

     When one staff member revealed that he did not believe he needed
cultural diversity training, Ashley directed the rest of the group to
imagine the "gall" it took to make such a statement. This type of
insipid intolerance shows how, in the name of "sensitivity," coerced
indoctrination has become an official part of university policy.

     Nearly half a century ago, the distinguished political philosopher
Hannah Arendt described the difference between authoritarian and
totalitarian dictatorships by showing that only the totalitarian variety
blurred the distinction between the private and the public life.3 Petty
authoritarian dictators required and demanded political acquiescence,
not participation. Totalitarian dictators, in contrast, did not permit
political neutrality. No matter how banal and meaningless the ritual--
like voting for a single candidate for office in the Soviet Union--one
had to participate or face the consequences. At the University of
Cincinnati, library staff members learned that they could not simply be
neutral captives of banality, but they were compelled, as victims of the
militant Left's new totalism, to actively participate and think as they
were told, no matter how fatuous the ideas presented to them.

     Participation can be a strong inducement to change attitudes
through the psychological mechanism known as "commitment," at least when
the illusion of voluntary participation is maintained. The Chinese
Communists were masters of such techniques. Their indoctrination experts
were able to convince even prisoners of war that participation in their
own "political re-education" was voluntary.4 The University of
Cincinnati administration's attempt at indoctrination was not so expert.
Fortunately, Vice Provost Ashley's use of coercion to obtain compliance
undermined the commitment she tried to achieve. "Doing" can be
psychologically effective. "Doing" can produce commitment that bonds
people to behavior. But coerced "doing" achieves only resentment. The
group leader only superficially understood the psychological mechanics
of commitment. But the issue is not her failure to understand properly
what the Communist Chinese so cleverly practiced in the 1950s. The
question that should gnaw at all of us is: What is a university, in a
free society, doing practicing this kind of mind-manipulation on its
employees? Is there any goal that could remotely justify these means?
Could the University of Cincinnati go before the citizens of Ohio and
say, "Yes, these means are totalitarian, but just look at what we hope
to achieve with them?"

     Since it is obvious that these sessions could not possibly produce
attitude change, what is their real function? On an economic level, they
provide justification for an entire level of bureaucracy that otherwise
could not hope to exist. They provide a claim to professional expertise
without the rigors of any objective testing or independent evaluation.

     On a psychological level, these sessions represent the politics of
vengeance. The one continual theme that emerges from all the discussions
about sensitivity sessions I have had, whether with students or faculty,
whether on my campus or on others, is that at some point the sessions
degenerate into the public humiliation of whites. Sometimes the object
of humiliation is only white males; at other times it can be any white
person, male or female. The sensitivity sessions are designed to turn
the larger social system upside down. As we will see below, what is
valued in the larger society is the object of opprobrium in the
sensitivity sessions. The rules do change, and rather dramatically.
Those who have objective status are made to feel that their status is
ill-gained, the function of being privileged in a society built on
greed, exploitation, and oppression. Here, in microcosm, is nothing less
than the psychological gratification celebrated in Frantz Fanon's paean
for violence as the existential liberation of the oppressed.5 What Fanon
sought in third-world revolutions is being achieved within our
universities, at public expense, with psychological violence replacing
Fanon's demand for physical violence.

     As Fanon notes, "The naked truth of decolonization evokes for us
the searing bullets and bloodstained knives which emanate from it. For
if the last shall be first [if the oppressed shall replace the
oppressor], this will only come to pass after a murderous and decisive
struggle between the two protagonists. That affirmed intention to place
the last at the head of all things...can only triumph if we use all
means to turn the scale, including, of course that of violence." 6

     Jean-Paul Sartre is most responsible for placing Fanon's violence
of liberation into existential terms. Exhorting his fellow Europeans to
come to grips with Fanon's gory vision of the future, Sartre notes how
Fanon "shows clearly that his irrepressible violence is neither sound
and fury, nor the resurrection of savage instincts, nor even the effect
of resentment: it is man recreating himself. . . . When his rage boils
over, he rediscovers his lost innocence and he comes to himself in that
he himself creates his self."7

     Paralleling Fanon, the previously oppressed, with the full
support, sustenance and authority of the State, have now become the
"first," and those who were once "first" are now the "last." Their new
status is affirmed by the public humiliation they receive and silently
suffer. It is not upon pain of death or even through threat of physical
discomfort that they are cowered, but through a strange alchemy of guilt
and reflexive obedience to authority. Their status is inexplicable in
light of their current trembling. As the sensitivity expert transforms
himself through the politics of vengeance into the first, those who were
once first bay like so many sheep, supplicants to their own fear,
passively confirming the sensitivity expert's new-found status and self-
importance.

     Such fear is understandable for students, without the compass of
experience to navigate through the strange and abusive psychological
ritual of sensitivity training. But how does one explain the behavior of
faculty?  What is most inexplicable is that faculty often simply sit by
with nothing more than idle nervousness while one of their own is
grotesquely humiliated.

     On the campus where I teach, one of the most notorious of these
episodes occurred at a session for faculty in the University College,
the university's open admissions college. With classes canceled, Dr.
Edwin J. Nichols, a sensitivity expert, was flown in from Washington,
D.C. The college dean, David Hartleb (now Provost), praised Nichols as
perhaps the best sensitivity expert in America.  By organizing and
publicizing this session, the college was demonstrating to its faculty
that it took seriously its commitment to racial sensitivity.

     In a large hall in the Tangeman University Center, Nichols asked
all the women to stand up. He then asked each one to announce where she
had received her undergraduate degree. Those with degrees from
prestigious universities were asked to remain standing. The process was
repeated for the men and then again for both women and men with respect
to graduate degrees.

     At the end of the selection, in a room full of over one hundred
people, one young woman possessed the qualifications that kept her
standing before her peers. She had attained all three degrees at
prestigious private schools. Her undergraduate degree came from
Wellesley and her graduate degrees from Harvard and New York University.
By any conventional standard in the academy, this is quite a list of
academic accomplishments, something of which one should be proud. But
not in the "newthink" world being built on Fanon's existential concept
of vengeance where the first shall be last and the last first.

     "This is a member of the privileged white elite," the sensitivity
expert intoned to the audience, pointing to the young woman as if she
were some anthropological specimen available for public viewing. She, a
graduate of the Seven Sisters with blonde hair and blue eyes, is the
most likely person in the room to succeed, he continued.

     It was as if there were something illegitimate about her
accomplishment and something that unnaturally set her apart from the
rest of the audience. No mention was made of the work it takes to get
into such schools, let alone to get through them. The implied message
was that such education comes naturally, as some sort of privilege for
society's white elite and that, perhaps, an elite education was not only
an illegitimate acquisition, but a genetic entitlement expressly
reserved for those with blonde hair and blue eyes.

     The young woman, who had been at the University of Cincinnati all
of three weeks, was forced to remain standing through this diatribe. No
one jumped up to point out that such an education comes from a
combination of mental aptitude and hard work and that the woman's
appearance had nothing to do with her accomplishments--that being blonde
and blue-eyed contained neither the genetic nor the social code for
educational destiny! The professorate of fear fidgeted nervously but
remained as silent as lambs witnessing the slaughter of their own kind.

     There came a welcome break in the session, and for the young woman
who was publicly showcased, it was, she thought, an end to the ordeal.
But in the world of the politics of vengeance, there was more to come.
When the group reconvened, the expert in sensitivity announced the group
would have a beauty contest. They would vote on the most beautiful woman
among them.

     But then, instantly, Nichols said that he had reconsidered. Such a
contest was unnecessary, for they all knew who the most beautiful woman
among them was. She had just been standing before them, he continued.
She was the blonde, blue-eyed young woman with the Seven Sisters
pedigree.

     He then asked her to stand up so that everyone could look at her
again. By this time, she was riveted to her chair in embarrassment.
Nichols kept telling her to stand up, but she remained immobilized,
sobbing in response to his taunts.

     In a room of some one hundred well-educated people, no one rose to
the woman's defense. No one sought to put an end to the scenario of
vengeance. Instead, the professoriate of fear squirmed. To let the
audience know what they were being deprived of by the woman remaining
seated, the sensitivity expert called everyone's attention to what a
perfect specimen she was of the privileged elite for "she was even
wearing her string of pearls."

     Afterward, the lambs came forward to express to her their
sympathies for her humiliation, but no one came forward to complain
officially or publicly. Yet, incredibly enough, even after this episode,
Dean Hartleb invited Nichols back to the University of Cincinnati.
Several years have elapsed and people are still fearful of discussing
the episode. Such is the manner in which the politically correct
intimidate and control their opposition.

     Nichols' approach to the sensitivity session is strikingly similar
to one that was used, for a short while, in the late 1980s in Great
Britain. There, whites were seated and surrounded by their non-white
colleagues and urged to confess their racism. When the confessions did
not achieve the standards deemed appropriate by the sensitivity trainer,
the non-whites screamed at and taunted their white colleagues. After
several whites had nervous collapses as a result of these sessions, an
aghast Margaret Thatcher stepped in to not only stop the sessions, but
to eliminate the entire organ of government that was responsible for
funding them.8

     The politics of vengeance is also visible in other sensitivity
sessions held at the University of Cincinnati, especially those for
students and staff who work in the campus dormitories. Clearly, then,
university administrators have either different agendas or different
constituencies to which they are responding, for it is within such
sessions that one can see Fanon's philosophical ravings transformed into
policy.

     How do such programs persist in the face of negative consequences?
Sadly, universities and colleges have failed to comprehend the lessons
Alexis de Tocqueville so brilliantly taught in his characterization of
the Old Regime on the eve of the French Revolution. Institutions and
systems of preference built on ascription instead of achievement
collapse from the weight of their own corruption. First they invite
incompetence and then they invite a resentment that inevitably swells
into outrage.9

     Furthermore, as powerful political forces demand that universities
do something about racial conflict, the universities are able to respond
that they have hired sensitivity consultants who are engaged in
conducting awareness sessions and workshops. A "sensitivity bureaucracy"
develops inside the system, providing patronage positions for minorities
whose life experiences are seen as equipping them with unchallengeable,
if not incomparable, qualifications in understanding cultural
differences. The underlying and unassailable assumption is that all
members of a minority group experience the same subcultural experiences
in the same way. Ethnicity and race are taken to subsume the experiences
of region and even class, although no one has marshalled one shred of
data to justify that supposition. Furthermore, it is easier to confer
expertise in racial awareness than in academic disciplines, and campus
administrations have found them to be a convenient mechanism for meeting
the goals and timetables of the federal record-keepers tallying minority
progress in the institution.

     Both within and outside the university, a whole cadre of
sensitivity experts has emerged. Self-anointed, mutually proclaimed as
experts, they are available, for a fee, of course, to conduct workshops
outside their own universities and will exorcise the demon of
"insensitivity," and even expose racist practices of which no one was
ever previously aware.

     Edwin J. Nichols, who humiliated the blonde, blue-eyed instructor
at Ohio taxpayers' expense, charges $2,500, plus expenses, for a half-
day workshop. If the workshop requires the participation of one of his
associates, the price climbs another $2,000. For those colleges and
universities suffering from tenacious, demonic, racist possession,
Nichols will conduct a more extensive "exorcism," which he calls a
"cultural audit." The fee runs anywhere from $8,000 to $16,000.

     Nearly all colleges and universities have experts who can easily
apply the methodology of research to assess the validity of such
workshops and whether the benefits justify the excessive costs, yet no
such evaluations occur.

     In a political climate where pressure groups are demanding that
something be done to alleviate racial tensions, universities are
reluctant to call into question the very mechanisms those same pressure
groups offer as solutions. To do so would raise the issue of the
justification for and legitimacy of the entire sensitivity industry and
race-oriented bureaucracy. Such displays of public candor would force
colleges and universities to admit that not only is their role of
attitude commissar questionable in a society where speech is protected
and thought is inviolable, but that people, even under conditions of
extreme coercion, are unlikely to change attitudes that they do not want
to change.

     Colleges and universities now admit that after seventy years of
the most sophisticated attempts at Pavlovian-based mind control ever
exerted on a population, the Soviet government, built on a lie, could
not survive the harsh exposure to truth. Would these institutions have
us believe that what the Soviets could not accomplish with their mixture
of coercion and psychological persuasion over three generations, the
sensitivity trainers, such as Mary Ellen Ashley and Edwin J. Nichols,
can achieve in hours? The answer they are giving is "yes." This is the
charade being perpetrated on the college community and the public. This
is the politics of deception and vengeance behind which college
administrators are hiding. They are attempting to secure short-term
respite from the intense pressure of interest group politics that is
demanding outcomes that neither universities nor anyone else can
produce.

     Those embarking on  such schemes  might well remember that
minority rights and liberties are only secure as long as the majority is
willing to recognize the legitimacy of the minorities' political claims.
Civil rights became a reality in this country because whites were
willing to link arms with blacks and put their own bodies and lives on
the line. The impetus for that sacrifice was the belief that people
should be judged by the content of their character and not the color of
their skin. When minorities raise skin color as the ultimate basis for
entitlement, they throw into question their hard-won claims to justice
based on the rights of individuals.

     As colleges and universities attempt to inculcate notions of group
and statistical justice, plied through the corrupt politics of
vengeance, and sustained by narrow-sighted, race-based interest group
pressure, we may find ourselves in a society that easily recognizes
group differences but has lost the respect for the ideal of individual
justice that enables minority rights to have its ultimate moral claim on
legitimacy.

