RE: Hate speech laws

In the U.S. hate speech is generally constitutionally protected, so
neither the state of Wisconsin or the university can punish it. This
was confirmed on June 23, last year in the Wisconsin State Supreme
court case _Wisconsin v Mitchell_, in the U.S. Supreme court case _RAV
v. St. Paul, and in the federal district court case _UWM Post v. U. of
Wisconsin_ (see refs).

RE: Violating netiquette

In the U.S., state agents generally lack authority to enforce
etiquette. Speech activity can be punished for being harassing or for
being substanitally disruptive, but not mjst for being rude and
offensive. The rational is that "rudeness" is so subjective that
giving the government the authority to punishment would give it
authority to punish almost anything. (see refs).

- Carl

