Net '93 Report. Wqshington, DC April 14-16, 1993

This EDUCOM conference was the same venue that Gore revealed his NREN
plan in 1988. The mood was upbeat because of the change in
Administration, and yet the issues and the turmoil caused by
commercial interests in the NREN/NII, access issues for the
undeserved, how suitable the Net is for school kids, how the Net will
scale up for many millions of users, plus a good dose of new
technology, made this an exciting conference.

Eric Benhamou of 3Com did the keynote. He characterized the Internet
as a very special network where there is a high tolerance for
problems, and where disasters are seen as subjects for a thesis, not
as a prompt to switch service providers.  The commercial networks are
much different.  He sees the NII as a great parade, but the real
issues are things like user training, deploying a complex router in a
school, small business, or library where there is little technical
expertise.  At present, there is no compelling need by the user, so
he as a businessman won't venture into it.

The opening panel was very useful; it presented the views of CSPP,
EFF, the telcos--via Bellcore, and the non-profit sector as
represented by Rick Weingarten.  Ken Kay of CSPP talked about
Clinton's trip to Silicon Valley and how Gore needs recognition for
his foresight to promote this over the past 12 years. Kay says there
has to be a conscious link between the HPCCI and the NII; you can't
separate the high end stuff from the bigger issues of NII. He is
worried that the administration will focus more on telecom
regulations than on the human resources of the NII. the CSPP CEOs
have told them that networking is a management intensive activity,
and the coordination office is not enough. The network has to be
physically managed with milestones and a five year plan. He also said
that security and privacy issues were first order impediments. The
govt. is worried about dialog with all the groups because they fear
there will be a 'food fight that they cannot control' (as with the
cable/telco King Kong/Godzilla battle). He said that non-governmental
input (corporate, academic, and civic) needs to break the mold.

John Barlow said he feels like the dog that caught the car. Now what
to do with this huge issue. He said the Internet is like a slime
mold, a living, growing form. He said give the NII plenty of
nutrients and stand out of the way.  (But who does the giving, and
who stands out of the way? The Government, Private Industry? It was
not clear to me.) He portrayed the cable and telcos as at war, but
did not mention the recent deals being cut by previously warring
parties.  He asked how do we have rapid deployment of narrowband, and
said ISDN could be the ramp up to fiber.  He supported subsidies for
schools and the disadvantaged, but also said that users, not
institutions should get the subsidy. The mechanism for this sort of
distribution was not spelled out.

Tom Spacek, executive director for NII initiatives, Bellcore. He
showed a diagram of rockets labeled telco, Internet (both large), and
satellite, wireless, and cable (in the background). He said it is
critical for these forces to come together to push for the NII. He
characterized the Internet as small compared to the 1 billion
telecomms users on commercial networks and said the reliability is a
big problem. He said the Internet is very nimble when it comes to
standards, whereas the telcos have been very slow to change. His most
provocative claim was "When the government subsidizes networks, it is
a disincentive to the private sector." The needs of entrepreneurship,
research, and the public good clash. He said government networks
should not provide services to private users. <This was the day that
the telcos told Gore what they'd do to build the NII: put in many
billions in exchange for non restrictions on long distance service,
acquisition of cable companies, and manufacturing of equipment.>

Rick Weingarten, exec. director of the computing research assn. and
former network guru at OTA, said the Net is a bunch of tinker toys;
it can do just about anything but it's hard to configure. The old
communication path between the user and the provider was the
regulatory path. Now they are pulling back and are using the market
approach. The sophisticated users know how to roll their own
services, but there is less likelihood of universal service because
there are so many different offerings in the digital world.

Most important he said, "Public needs do not create markets." He
objected strongly to Spacek's claim that subsidy is the word for
telecom services purchased by the government. He said it is
appropriate for a lawyer to use the city supported Metro system when
she is going to visit a client or when a business person uses a
library to get information to improve his business. So it's okay for
individuals to use the Net for a variety of uses. <Dave Hughes doing
business as a vendor with contractors in Russia is a good example of
net use he would say is okay, I think>

Carol Henderson of the American Library Assn. chaired a panel that
included Dennis Conners of the Public Telecommunications Facilities
Program at NTIA. The $54,000,000 for 1994 will be funneled through
his office and from 95-98 would have another $150 million. Most of
these projects will be 'last mile efforts.' They are looking more at
non-profit projects than for profit (schools, libraries, health).

Jeff Chester, Center for Media Education, pursued the need for public
input into the shift in the world of networks and media service. Jeff
is oriented toward television and does not use the Net at this time,
but he raised the specter of 500 channels with nothing much better on
than what we have today. He showed a video from TV Answer with some
gleeful consumers, presumable already digitally lobotomized into a
dreamy state of acceptance of a world that scares Chester. He kept
trying to start and then stop the video, but the audience wanted to
see more of it. He said we need non-profit rates for non-profit
producers, and that there needs to be a better debate on telecomm
issues as there is on the environment and health issues.

Mike Nelson, OSTP and Gore's most knowledgeable scientist, said he
spent most of his time on NII issues. He repeated what Clinton had
said about Gore, "It really helps to have a vice-president who
understands the gestalt of the gigabit." The admin's main goals:
promote economic jobs that protect the environment, make the
government more responsive and more efficient using info technology
and energy use, maintain leadership in basic science and math by
restoring NSF funding to move ahead on all fronts. Increased Dept. of
Commerce advanced technology program through matching grants. For a
world-class business environment they want permanent research credit.
Use education technology to reach out to more students and teachers.
He stressed the information on-ramp program to provide connections to
libraries, schools, and non-profits throughout the country. It won't
put a computer in every class room but at least in almost every
school.

He said telecomms and info policy are all screwed up. The 1950's regs
hold back the 1990's technologies. There will be a task force run by
the Natl. Economic Council, chaired by Jack Gibbons. Evolution of the
Internet, cable/telco, and wireless issues are the most important.
Info policy: how to make federal data more available, while
protecting privacy and security of users?  OMB and NTIA will be
involved here.

I ran a panel on community networks. Speakers included Diane Kovacs
who ran a variety of conferences on Bitnet for librarians and
scholars, Richard Civille of Center for Civic Networking, who spoke
about the conference CCN convened earlier that week. Anne Fallis from
South Dakota was assisted by George Baldwin as she talked about and
he showed Indian art work from the BBSes in Montana and Rapid City.
Anne made a very good impression on the audience, and many people
responded to her request for help with the Indian schools. I'm glad
that she got exposure at this conference, and Mike Roberts said in
the closing statements that he was glad my panel had reached out to
new groups in the networking community.

There were demo rooms with a new Internet Cruise from Merit, neat
video applications including the public domain program from Cornell
called CU--see me, that allows multiple video conferences to take
place using cheap cameras and frame grabbing boards on the Mac.
George Brett showed off all the neat Internet tools, and Cornell also
showed some electronic books indexed in WAIS.  You search the
abstract and than read the bitmapped images of the books captured by
the Xerox Docutech system.  Very nice integration of some high-end
applications.

Sex on the Internet: Discussion of objectionable material on users'
screen such as digital video of a writhing nude woman in view of all
users in the work environment, or when another woman began receiving
objectionable pictures on her screen from someone else. The
guidelines were aimed at higher ed. institutions and some mention was
made of the K12 issues, but most of the speakers felt there should be
no censorship but more training of the users. One person said there
was a PG rated gopher for high school kids; they could not get out to
the controversial news groups which was, along with X-rated GIF
archives, the main troubling sources of information.  The University
of Michigan, CNI, and American University all had reps. speaking, and
I was impressed that all had personal limits of comfort with some of
the material on the Net, but that none wanted to impose their views
on the rest of the users.  First amendment purists did not speak up,
nor did those who say net administrators should not act 'in loco
parentis' Practically speaking, the kids know more about the net than
their parents, and yet the presence of X-rated material on K12
machines is a real threat to continued support of a program in a
public school.  Librarians can help net folks with some of these
issues: handling calls for censorship or removal of material. It was
interesting that one of the attendees had some rather rank material
he had ftp'ed and was gleefully (I thought) showing the graphic video
to a few people behind closed doors.  Intellectual interest and all
that!

Steve Wolff, head of networking for the NSF, gave the final address
at Net '93. He described his current focus on what he calls the
Information Well, that place in a community where people go for
information. This includes the libraries, the schools, and the
extension service--all institutions that have a tradition of service
to the people, even if they have  not yet  incorporated
telecommunications. His idea is that first you have a "well"
connected to the Internet/NREN  and then the individual plumbing
system is developed to bring services to homes and businesses.

There were many other sessions I did not cover or attend, but this
will give you a flavor of what I thought was interesting.

Kudos to Elizabeth Barnhardt of EDUCOM (and to Mike Roberts) and the
organizing committee for putting on a smooth-running and interesting
conference.  Very good AV/computer support too!

Steve Cisler Apple Library sac@apple.com





