Message-ID: <1993May9.203117.7551@cnsvax.uwec.edu>
Date: 9 May 93 20:31:17 -0600

Okay, here's the deal:

The university set up rules governing the use of their system. They own the
Internet nameservers and equipment, and so they have the final say in how their
eqipment is used. At UW-Eau Claire, the usage should be related to and should
not conflict with the University's stated goals. I know because network gaming
(MUDs) got out of hand last November, people complained, and we are now
banned from using University hardware for ANY games Sun-Fri 6am-11pm. 
...according to this McElwaine disagreement, these game players have a RIGHT to
use the network, and so they should be able to continue without the University
having any say in what the game players use the equipment for.

Note that the network here is treated as a resource for students and faculty.
If this resource is abused (like with the gaming), it can be taken away.

Not all students have network access... they must fill out a form requesting
an account first on the VAX, and then they can use the Internet or system.
Because of this, I feel that the students do not have a 'right' to use the
system and have an account.. they must ask to be allowed to use the system and
resources first. This, IMHO, makes the University the supreme authority on
system use, since they are allowing access.
                                                                              
-Dale Mahalko
mahalkdp@cnsvax.uwec.edu

Note: I am only a student here.. these are my own opinions and may not reflect
the opinion of the UW system or UW-Eau Claire. What I write here is based on my
own observations and experiences over this year.

