   I don't think that is an effective solution. Sometimes a followup
   with a large repost content is appropriate, sometimes not. I hate
   to reread a 6-screen article at 2400 bd just to get to the original
   one-liner at the end just as much as anyone, but I don't see any way
   to tell when such reposting is important to making the poster's
   point. On the other hand, is it ever appropriate to put separate
   copies of a post to many unrelated groups, instead of a single
   crossposted copy? McE. is surely aware that this practice is not
   only a waste of resources but also completely unnecessary; yet
   he persists in doing so.  Worse yet, he does it over and over 
   again with the same tired old tripe (literally...the same articles
   reposted over and over).

   BUT---Nonetheless, I say let him keep his access. The resources
   available to the net _seem_ so vast that I can't imagine Bob
   is really straining them. I hate, hate, hate ANYthing that smells
   even remotely like censorship. In this case it is probably the
   content of his posts that gets Bob noticed (after all, he is 
   obviously starved for attention and can only get noticed by being
   such an annoying little netnik), but the way he crossreposts
   and recrossposts would seem to be the real reason for his
   loss of access. Is that censorship? Little more so than refusing
   to print an obscenity-filled letter in a newspaper if the same
   issues can be raised and arguments advanced without such language
   (In fairness, the issue in Bob's case is a bit more ambiguous).
   I don't argue in favor of locking him out of the net, but I
   think that to equate the situation with censorship due to 
   content is ridiculous.

   Consider: Joe Schmoe writes a script that auto-reposts a perfectly
   reasonable post to a single, appropriate newsgroup...once every
   three minutes for two weeks. Even without considering content, it
   seems quite correct to tell Joe he must stop or lose his access.

   Now, make it once an hour...still OK to punish Joe?
   Now once daily...or weekly...at some point it is going to
   be ambiguous whether or not Joe should be stopped, but it
   is a matter of degree. Bob McE. is doing the same thing,
   except the posts go everywhere.
   
